## C. Sample Short Answer Questions — Quick Answer Guide in Order

### 1. Shenkman’s five characteristics
Wooden-headedness, shortsightedness, bone-headedness, cult of ignorance, negligence. Define each, give examples, say which you’re guilty of, and which is most pervasive/problematic in the U.S. today.

### 2. McRaney & Resnick — intellectual humility
Naïve realism, cognitive dissonance, confirmation bias, third person effect, Dunning-Kruger. Best arguments: everyone is biased; humility improves learning/deliberation; democracy needs fallibilism. Say whether convincing and why.

### 3. Government/democracy; direct vs. representative
Government: institutions making/enforcing collective decisions; necessary for public goods, order, rights. Democracy: rule by people; differs from monarchy/oligarchy/theocracy/authoritarianism. Direct: citizens vote on laws; pros/cons. Representative: elect reps; pros/cons. U.S.: representative at national level; WA has direct (initiatives/referenda) + representative. “We’re a republic, not a democracy”: a republic is a form of representative democracy; terms aren’t mutually exclusive.

### 4. Conservativism, liberalism, libertarianism, authoritarianism
Compare on economic and social/cultural issues. Conservatism: free market/tradition. Liberalism: regulation/equality. Libertarianism: free market/personal freedom. Authoritarianism: state control/limited freedoms. Note similarities/differences from World’s Smallest Political Quiz.

### 5. Great Compromise & 3/5ths Compromise
Great Compromise: Virginia Plan (large states, population) vs. New Jersey Plan (small states, equal) → bicameral: House by population, Senate equal. 3/5ths: enslaved counted as 3/5 for representation/taxation; South wanted 5/5, North 0/5. Impacted: enslaved people treated as property; small/large states. Legacy: Senate overrepresents small states; 3/5ths gave South extra EC power.

### 6. Amendment process
Proposal: 2/3 Congress or 2/3 state convention. Ratification: 3/4 state legislatures or conventions. Logic: stability, consensus, protects small states. Downsides: hard to fix undemocratic features; outdated. Argue whether easier and how.

### 7. Separation of powers & checks and balances
Separation: legislative makes law; executive enforces; judicial interprets. Checks: veto, override, impeachment, Senate confirmation, judicial review. Give multiple concrete examples.

### 8. Undemocratic elements of Constitution
Original (≥3): slavery/3/5ths; Senate equal representation; Electoral College; no direct Senate election; property requirements. Current (≥2 different): Electoral College; Senate malapportionment; filibuster; gerrymandering; voter suppression; DC/territories no voting representation.

### 9. FPTP vs. alternative voting (C.P. Grey)
FPTP: plurality wins; problems: spoiler effect, wasted votes, safe seats, two-party lock. Alternative vote/RCV: rank candidates; majority winner; no spoiler; more choice; less negative campaigning. Say whether you’d support change and why.

### 10. Electoral College
How: 538 electors; state electors = House + Senate; winner-take-all in most states; 270 to win. For: small states, federalism, stability. Against: popular-vote loser can win (2000, 2016); swing-state focus; racial bias/3/5ths legacy; faithless electors. Undemocratic features: indirect, unequal weighting, winner-take-all. Abolish/reform/keep? Argue—National Popular Vote Plan, RCV, etc.

### 11. Directly elected then vs. now
1787: only House directly elected. Senate by state legislatures; president by EC; judges appointed. Today: House and Senate directly elected (17th Amendment); president still EC; judges appointed.

### 12. Authoritarian vs. democratic executive; Trump
Authoritarian executive: centralizes power, attacks press/elections, suppresses opposition. Trump examples to weigh: “enemy of the people” press attacks; election lies; Jan. 6; court packing; attacking institutions. Be specific about which tactics fair/unfair to associate with him.

### 13. Newman — how democracy should/actually works
Should: equal representation, majority rule, accountability. Actually: rigged rules—Senate, EC, gerrymandering, filibuster, money, suppression. “Unrig the rules”: structural reforms. Say whether convincing and why.

### 14. Barriers to bill becoming law
Committee death; filibuster/60 votes; partisan polarization; presidential veto; House/Senate differences; gerrymandering/safe seats. Explain how each blocks bills.

### 15. House vs. Senate differences
Membership: 435 vs. 100. Terms: 2 vs. 6 years. Representation: population vs. equal states. Powers: House revenue/impeachment; Senate confirms/ratifies/tries impeachment. Rules: House majority power; Senate filibuster. Leadership: Speaker vs. Majority Leader.

### 16. Small-state overrepresentation
Legislative: Senate—Wyoming = California. Executive: Electoral College—small states get minimum 3 electors; votes per person higher in small states.

### 17. Filibusters
Senate only; extended debate; 60 votes cloture to end. For: minority rights/deliberation. Against: undemocratic, blocks majority, used against civil rights. Give your view.

### 18. Gerrymandering
Manipulating district lines for partisan advantage; packing/cracking; House and state legislatures. Problem: undemocratic, safe seats, polarization. Best fix: independent commissions, proportional representation, RCV.

### 19. D.C./territories — taxation without representation
D.C. 700k+ pay taxes, no voting Congress representation; territories like Puerto Rico/Guam no voting representation. Legacy of empire and Constitution. Change? Statehood/amendment. Argue.

### 20. Voter suppression
Historical barriers (≥5): poll taxes, literacy tests, grandfather clauses, white primaries, property requirements, race/gender exclusion. Current barriers (≥3): voter ID, felon disenfranchisement, poll closures, purges, gerrymandering. Changes: automatic registration, mail-in, restore felon rights, independent redistricting, holiday. Argue.

### 21. Newman — single-member districts/winner-take-all
SMD + winner-take-all → uncompetitive districts, polarization, apathy, two-party lock. PR/RCV → better representation, more parties, higher turnout. Say whether you agree and why.

### 22. Jan. 6 film — “Lies, Politics and Democracy”
What: Trump supporters stormed Capitol to stop Biden certification; deaths/injuries/damage. Contributing: Trump’s lies, GOP complicity, social media, polarization, institutional failure. Significance today: election denialism, distrust, threat of violence, democratic backsliding. Show you watched film: specific people/scenes/quotes.

### 23. Whitewashing U.S. history
Arguments: omission of slavery/genocide/racism; textbook sanitizing; 1619 Project controversy; Confederate monuments. Say whether convincing and why, with specifics.

### 24. Bullshit vs. lying
Bullshit: persuasion without regard for truth (Frankfurt). Lying: intentional falsehood. Problematic: erodes trust, truth becomes irrelevant, polarizes, harms democracy. Why so much: social media incentives, polarization, profit, attention economy. Recent examples: election lies, COVID misinformation.

### 25. Cognitive offloading/atrophy/foreclosure; AI
Offloading: outsourcing thinking. Atrophy: weakening from disuse. Foreclosure: premature certainty. Assigned materials suggest which is bigger for younger vs. older people—be specific from readings. AI contributes by making offloading easy and reducing critical thinking. Say whether convincing and why.

